/**
 * Define a named world extension.
 *
 * Extensions are runtime-only capabilities attached to a World. They may
 * register event listeners, adapters, runtimes, virtuals, diagnostics, or
 * other world-local behavior. They are not serialized.
 *
 * The installer function may return a cleanup function.
 *
 * @param {string} name
 * @param {(world: World) => (void|Function)} installer
 * @param {{ key?: any }} [options]
 * @returns {{ key:any, name:string, install:(world:World)=>(void|Function) }}
 */
export function defineExtension(name, installer, options = {}) {
  if (typeof name !== "string" || !name) {
    throw new Error("defineExtension: name must be a non-empty string");
  }

  if (typeof installer !== "function") {
    throw new Error("defineExtension: installer must be a function");
  }

  const key = Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(options, "key")
    ? options.key
    : Symbol(name);

  return Object.freeze({
    key,
    name,
    install: installer
  });
}
```

## Why `Symbol(name)` Instead of `name` as the Key?

Extension identity should be based on the extension object, not a loose string.

Two packages can both have an extension named `"audio"`.

The default `Symbol(name)` gives each extension a unique identity while preserving a readable name for diagnostics.

If a caller wants a globally stable key, they can supply one:

```js
export const audioBridge = defineExtension(
  "audioBridge",
  world => {
    // attach bridge
  },
  { key: Symbol.for("my-package:audioBridge") }
);
```

## Accepted Install Inputs

`world.install(...)` should accept either a canonical extension object:

```js
const extension = defineExtension("debugLog", world => {
  // setup
});
```

or a function extension for compatibility and terse one-off setup:

```js
function debugLog(world) {
  // setup
}
```

Canonical form is `defineExtension(...)`.

Function extensions are supported for incremental migration.

## World Internal State

Add an extension registry to `World`.

In the constructor:

```js
this._extensions = new Map();
```

The map should be:

```txt
Map<extensionKey, {
  key,
  name,
  extension,
  uninstall
}>
```

This registry is runtime-only.

It must not be included in snapshots.

## Helper Functions

Add near `defineExtension(...)` or near the `World` class.

```js
function extensionKey(extension) {
  if (!extension) {
    throw new Error("install: extension required");
  }

  if (typeof extension === "function") {
    return extension;
  }

  if (typeof extension === "object") {
    if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(extension, "key")) {
      return extension.key;
    }

    return extension;
  }

  throw new Error("install: expected function or extension object");
}

function extensionName(extension) {
  if (typeof extension === "function") {
    return extension.name || "<anonymous extension>";
  }

  if (extension && typeof extension === "object") {
    return String(extension.name || "<anonymous extension>");
  }

  return "<invalid extension>";
}

function runExtensionInstaller(extension, world) {
  if (typeof extension === "function") {
    return extension(world);
  }

  if (extension && typeof extension.install === "function") {
    return extension.install(world);
  }

  throw new Error("install: expected function or object with install(world)");
}
```

## World API

Add these methods to `World`.

```js
/**
 * Attach a world extension once.
 *
 * Extension functions and extension objects may return a cleanup function.
 * Installing the same extension more than once is a no-op.
 *
 * @param {Function|{ key?:any, name?:string, install:(world:World)=>(void|Function) }} extension
 * @returns {this}
 */
install(extension) {
  const key = extensionKey(extension);

  if (this._extensions.has(key)) {
    return this;
  }

  const name = extensionName(extension);

  let cleanup = null;

  try {
    const result = runExtensionInstaller(extension, this);
    cleanup = typeof result === "function" ? result : null;
  } catch (e) {
    logError(`[ecs] extension "${name}" failed to install`, e);
    throw e;
  }

  this._extensions.set(key, {
    key,
    name,
    extension,
    uninstall: cleanup
  });

  return this;
}

/**
 * Remove a previously attached world extension.
 *
 * If the extension installer returned a cleanup function, it is invoked.
 *
 * @param {Function|{ key?:any, name?:string, install?:Function }} extension
 * @returns {boolean} true when something was removed
 */
uninstall(extension) {
  const key = extensionKey(extension);
  const rec = this._extensions.get(key);

  if (!rec) {
    return false;
  }

  this._extensions.delete(key);

  if (typeof rec.uninstall === "function") {
    try {
      rec.uninstall(this);
    } catch (e) {
      logError(`[ecs] extension "${rec.name}" uninstall failed`, e);
    }
  }

  return true;
}

/**
 * Check whether an extension is already attached.
 *
 * @param {Function|{ key?:any }} extension
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
hasExtension(extension) {
  const key = extensionKey(extension);
  return this._extensions.has(key);
}

/**
 * Return diagnostic records for attached extensions.
 *
 * @returns {Array<{ name:string, key:any, extension:any, uninstallable:boolean }>}
 */
extensions() {
  return Array.from(this._extensions.values()).map(rec => ({
    name: rec.name,
    key: rec.key,
    extension: rec.extension,
    uninstallable: typeof rec.uninstall === "function"
  }));
}
```

## Method Naming

Prefer:

```js
world.hasExtension(extension)
world.extensions()
```

over:

```js
world.isInstalled(extension)
world.installed()
```

Reason:

```txt
hasExtension / extensions describe world state.

isInstalled / installed describe lifecycle mechanics.
```

`install(...)` and `uninstall(...)` remain good because they are verbs.

## Important Semantics

### Installation Is Idempotent

This must be safe:

```js
world.install(extension);
world.install(extension);
world.install(extension);
```

The installer function runs once.

### Extensions Are Runtime-Only

Extension records are not serialized.

Snapshots describe world state, not attached runtime capabilities.

### `load(...)` and `applySnapshot(...)` Should Not Clear Extensions

Extension state should survive loading a new snapshot into the same world.

A world extension is a capability of the world object. It is not an entity/component fact.

### Extensions May Return Cleanup Functions

This allows listener cleanup:

```js
export const loggingBridge = defineExtension("loggingBridge", world => {
  const offDamage = world.on("damage", payload => {
    console.log(payload);
  });

  const offMove = world.on("moved", payload => {
    console.log(payload);
  });

  return () => {
    offDamage();
    offMove();
  };
});
```

Then:

```js
world.install(loggingBridge);
world.uninstall(loggingBridge);
```

### Extensions Should Be Synchronous

Do not support async extension installation in this change.

If an extension starts async work, that is the extension's responsibility.

The core lifecycle remains synchronous and deterministic.

### Extensions Should Not Be Phase-Aware Yet

No extension dependency graph.

No extension ordering rules.

No `before` / `after` for extensions.

Order is caller-defined:

```js
world.install(a);
world.install(b);
world.install(c);
```

## WorldBuilder Integration

Current builder behavior collects setup functions and invokes them during `build()`.

Change `WorldBuilder.build()` so it delegates to `world.install(extension)`.

